---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const calendly = 'https://calendly.com/contact-advised-seo/30min?back=1';
const jsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      "@id": "https://www.afrirank.fr/en/amazon-sells-my-products/#article",
      "headline": "Amazon is outranking your brand on Google: what you can win back (and what you won't)",
      "description": "Amazon outranking my brand on Google? What you can win back, what you won't, and the workaround strategy. No jargon.",
      "inLanguage": "en",
      "image": "https://www.afrirank.fr/images/article-amazon-colis.jpg",
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://www.afrirank.fr/en/amazon-sells-my-products/",
      "author": { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.advised-seo.fr/#francis", "name": "Francis Ndimba", "url": "https://www.afrirank.fr/en/about/" },
      "publisher": { "@id": "https://www.afrirank.fr/#business" }
    },
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      "itemListElement": [
        { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://www.afrirank.fr/en/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Resellers outranking your brand", "item": "https://www.afrirank.fr/en/resellers-capture-brand-traffic/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Amazon sells my products", "item": "https://www.afrirank.fr/en/amazon-sells-my-products/" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "mainEntity": [
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Should I sell on Amazon myself?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "That's a business trade-off, not an SEO one: volume and visibility versus margin and dependence. What's certain: if third parties are already selling your products there, being present directly gives you back control of the listing. But it doesn't fix your Google visibility — those are two separate workstreams." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Can I stop Amazon or third parties from selling my products?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "In the US, the first-sale doctrine means you generally can't stop a legitimate buyer from reselling your products; the same broadly applies in the UK/EU (exhaustion of rights). You can, however, structure your distribution upstream (selective distribution, reseller terms) — one for your lawyer. On the visibility side, the real answer isn't to forbid: it's to take back the traffic." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Does Amazon's Brand Registry protect my brand on Google?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "No. It protects your listings within Amazon (content, counterfeits). Your visibility on Google and in AI answers plays out entirely elsewhere: on your own site." } }
      ]
    }
  ]
});
---
<Base
  title="Amazon outranking my brand on Google: what can I do?"
  description="Amazon outranking my brand on Google? What you can win back, what you won't, and the workaround strategy. No jargon."
  lang="en"
  frPath="/amazon-vend-mes-produits/"
  enPath="/en/amazon-sells-my-products/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}>

<section class="hero hero--page wrap">
  <div class="hero-top">
    <nav class="breadcrumb reveal" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/en/">Home</a><span class="sep">›</span>
      <a href="/en/resellers-capture-brand-traffic/">Resellers outranking your brand</a><span class="sep">›</span>
      <span>Amazon sells my products</span>
    </nav>
    <span class="hero-eyebrow reveal"><img src="/images/icon-orange.png" alt="" /> Leak #1 · Marketplaces</span>
    <h1 class="reveal">Amazon is outranking your brand on Google: <em>what you can win back</em> (and what you won't)</h1>
    <p class="hero-sub reveal">You type the name of one of your products. First result: Amazon. Sometimes with your own listing, sometimes with a third-party reseller, sometimes with a "sponsored" competitor right next to it. The sale happens — but the margin, the customer data and the relationship slip away from you.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec" style="padding-top:clamp(24px,4vw,48px)">
  <div class="wrap article reveal">
    <p class="lead">Here's an honest read on what's recoverable, and how to <a href="/en/resellers-capture-brand-traffic/">take back your brand's traffic</a>.</p>

    <figure>
      <img src="/images/article-amazon-colis.jpg" alt="Stack of cardboard parcels ready to be shipped by a marketplace" loading="lazy" />
    </figure>

    <h2>Let's be clear first: what you won't win back</h2>
    <p>On a raw product query — "castor oil 100ml" — Amazon has a domain authority almost no brand site will ever catch up with. Anyone promising to "beat Amazon" on that ground is selling you a mirage. And that's fine, for one simple reason: that's not where your battle is.</p>

    <h2>The queries Amazon structurally cannot win against you</h2>
    <p>Amazon is a distribution machine. It has no answer for:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Advice</strong>: "best oil for dry 4C hair", "how to use chebe butter"</li>
      <li><strong>The ritual</strong>: "winter protective hair routine", "pre-poo oil treatment"</li>
      <li><strong>Comparing your lines</strong>: "[your line A] or [your line B] for a sensitive scalp"</li>
      <li><strong>Cultural content</strong>: an ingredient's origin, its place in a tradition, the right way to work with it</li>
    </ul>
    <p>On these queries, a marketplace listing is a non-answer. A brand that covers them with real expertise ranks — and intercepts the customer one step before purchase, while she's still choosing. That's where your legitimacy becomes a technical advantage, not just a story. These advice queries are also <a href="/en/seo-afro-haircare/">the ones generalists miss because they don't speak your community's language</a>.</p>

    <h2>Why your product page loses to theirs</h2>
    <p>Three causes come up in almost every case I analyse: a domain authority in a different league, an Amazon listing that looks more complete to Google (reviews, Q&A, structured data), and — more awkward — a brand product page that isn't using its own weapons: no reviews displayed, no usage content, a product name nobody types.</p>
    <p>Working out which of these causes weighs most on <em>your</em> site is precisely a diagnostic job — every case has its own priority.</p>

    <h2>The workaround strategy, in three moves</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Lock down your brand.</strong> On "[your brand] + product", you should be the one answering — not a marketplace. It's the hottest, most legitimate traffic to defend. See: <a href="/en/branded-serp/">take back your branded SERP</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Own the advice.</strong> The usage and choice queries listed above: Amazon will never be credible there, and they feed your product pages directly.</li>
      <li><strong>Give a reason to buy direct.</strong> Exclusives, sizes, loyalty perks: SEO brings the visit, the direct offer converts it.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The order and weight of each move depend on your situation — that's what the Diagnostic quantifies, query by query.</p>

    <h2>How much is this leak costing you, precisely?</h2>
    <p>Get an estimate in 60 seconds with <a href="/en/lost-organic-revenue-calculator/">the lost organic revenue calculator</a> — editable assumptions, result in euros.</p>
    <p>For the real number, based on your Search Console data and your actual queries: <a href="/en/seo-diagnostic/">the AfriRank Diagnostic</a>, €290 (~$310), credited toward your first month if you continue.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec" style="padding-top:0">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head reveal"><span class="kicker">Frequently asked questions</span><h2 class="sec-title">Your questions, my answers.</h2></div>
    <div class="faq">
      <div class="faq-item reveal"><button class="faq-q">Should I sell on Amazon myself? <span class="pm"></span></button><div class="faq-a"><p>That's a business trade-off, not an SEO one: volume and visibility versus margin and dependence. What's certain: if third parties are already selling your products there, being present directly gives you back control of the listing. But it doesn't fix your Google visibility — those are two separate workstreams.</p></div></div>
      <div class="faq-item reveal"><button class="faq-q">Can I stop Amazon or third parties from selling my products? <span class="pm"></span></button><div class="faq-a"><p>In the US, the first-sale doctrine means you generally can't stop a legitimate buyer from reselling your products; the same broadly applies in the UK/EU (exhaustion of rights). You can, however, structure your distribution upstream (selective distribution, reseller terms) — one for your lawyer. On the visibility side, the real answer isn't to forbid: it's to take back the traffic.</p></div></div>
      <div class="faq-item reveal"><button class="faq-q">Does Amazon's Brand Registry protect my brand on Google? <span class="pm"></span></button><div class="faq-a"><p>No. It protects your listings <em>within</em> Amazon (content, counterfeits). Your visibility on Google and in AI answers plays out entirely elsewhere: on your own site.</p></div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec" style="padding-top:0">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="links-more reveal">
      <h2>Go further</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/en/resellers-capture-brand-traffic/">The 4 traffic leaks of an established brand</a></li>
        <li><a href="/en/branded-serp/">Take back your branded SERP</a></li>
        <li><a href="/en/seo-afro-haircare/">The diaspora vocabulary generalists miss</a></li>
        <li><a href="/en/lost-organic-revenue-calculator/">Estimate your lost organic revenue</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="cta-band reveal">
      <img src="/images/icon-cream.png" class="icon-watermark" alt="" />
      <h2>Start with the Diagnostic.</h2>
      <p>The real number on what Amazon and the marketplaces capture in your place, based on your Search Console data — and the prioritised workaround plan. €290 (~$310), credited toward your first month if you continue.</p>
      <div class="cta-actions">
        <a href={calendly} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn--orange">Book my Diagnostic
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12h14M13 5l7 7-7 7"/></svg>
        </a>
        <a href="/en/seo-diagnostic/" class="btn btn--cream">Learn more</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</Base>
